$article=['date'=>'2027-01-13','slug'=>'sensory-seeking-child','tag'=>'Occupational Therapy','category'=>'Occupational Therapy & Sensory Processing','breadcrumb'=>'The Sensory-Seeking Child','meta_title'=>'The Sensory-Seeking Child: Crashing, Spinning and Chewing','description'=>'Understand why a sensory-seeking child may crash, spin or chew, how to observe the need behind the action and when occupational therapy may help your child.','h1'=>'The Sensory-Seeking Child: Why Some Kids Crash, Spin, or Chew on Everything','dek'=>'The action may be an attempt to gain movement, pressure or oral input, but support must fit the individual child and activity.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">A sensory-seeking child actively pursues input that helps them feel alert, organised or comfortable. They may jump, spin, crash, touch objects, make noise or chew. Similar actions can also reflect boredom, anxiety, habit, pain or ADHD, so observation matters.</p><h2>Map the action to context</h2><p>Record when it occurs, what happened before, how long it lasts and whether it improves participation. Chewing during difficult work may serve a different function from chewing caused by dental pain.</p><h2>Offer safer participation</h2><p>Provide regular movement opportunities, sturdy active play and safe oral alternatives appropriate to age and risk. Set boundaries around unsafe crashing while offering another way to meet the need.</p><h2>Avoid a copied sensory diet</h2><p>AOTA recommends documented assessment before sensory-based intervention. A strategy that alerts one child may overwhelm another, and equipment requires safe use.</p><h2>When OT can help</h2><p>Seek assessment when seeking interferes with learning, sleep, eating, safety or family activities. The goal is meaningful participation, not eliminating harmless self-regulation. Explore <a href="/occupational-therapy/sensory-seeking-behaviour">sensory-seeking support</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Is sensory seeking always ADHD or autism?','No. Sensory seeking can occur across different developmental profiles or without a diagnosis. Assessment considers context, function, safety and the child&rsquo;s broader development.'],['Why does my child chew clothing?','Chewing may provide oral input, support regulation or reflect habit, anxiety or discomfort. Persistent or unsafe chewing deserves individual assessment, including medical causes when relevant.'],['Should I stop my child from spinning?','Harmless movement need not automatically be stopped. Intervene for safety, distress or disrupted participation, and offer an appropriate alternative rather than relying only on prohibition.'],['What does an occupational therapist assess?','An OT examines sensory patterns in relation to daily activities, motor skills, environment, regulation and participation before recommending individualised strategies or equipment.']],
'cta_title'=>'Find safer ways to meet sensory needs','cta_text'=>'Explore an occupational therapy assessment linked to your child&rsquo;s daily participation.','cta_url'=>'/occupational-therapy/sensory-seeking-behaviour','cta_label'=>'Explore Sensory-Seeking Support']; require __DIR__.'/../components/scheduled-blog-renderer.php';
